      title: "David Walker's Appeal",
      body: `## A Boston clothier

In **September 1829**, a free Black man in **Boston** published a seventy-six-page pamphlet that terrified the slaveholding South. Its title was ***Walker's Appeal, in Four Articles; Together with a Preamble, to the Coloured Citizens of the World, but in Particular, and Very Expressly, to Those of the United States of America***. Its author, **David Walker** (c. 1796–1830), was born free in **Wilmington, North Carolina**, the son of an enslaved father and a free mother, and, since a child's status followed the mother's, he was free. He traveled through the South as a young man, witnessing slavery, and spent time in **Charleston**, South Carolina, where he may have known of the planned slave insurrection of **Denmark Vesey** in 1822. By the late 1820s, he had settled in Boston, where he ran a used-clothing shop near the docks, and became a leader of the Black community and an agent for *Freedom's Journal*, the first Black newspaper in the United States, founded in New York in **1827**.

## The Appeal

The *Appeal* was unlike anything written before. It was addressed not to white readers, pleading for their sympathy, but to Black readers, urging them to resist. It denounced slavery as the worst tyranny in history, worse than that of Egypt over Israel, because American slaveholders denied that Black people were human:

> "We, (coloured people of these United States of America) are the most wretched, degraded and abject set of beings that ever lived since the world began, and I pray God that none like us ever may live again until time shall be no more. … The Indians of North and of South America—the Greeks—the Irish, subjected under the king of Great Britain—the Jews, that ancient people of the Lord—the inhabitants of the islands of the sea—in fine, all the inhabitants of the earth … are called men, and of course are, and ought to be free. But we, (coloured people) and our children are *brutes*!!"

Walker attacked Jefferson's *Notes on the State of Virginia* directly, urging Black people to refute it by their own achievements: "Mr. Jefferson's remarks respecting us, have sunk deep into the hearts of millions of the whites, and never will be removed this side of eternity." He attacked the **American Colonization Society**, founded in 1816 to send free Black people to Africa (it founded **Liberia** in 1822), insisting that Black people had built America and had a right to it: "**America is more our country, than it is the whites—we have enriched it with our blood and tears.**"

## The right of resistance

Most alarming to slaveholders was Walker's call for resistance. He urged the enslaved not to submit, and, if attacked, to fight:

> "Remember Americans, that we must and shall be free and enlightened as you are, will you wait until we shall, under God, obtain our liberty by the crushing arm of power? Will it not be dreadful for you? … it is no more harm for you to kill a man, who is trying to kill you, than it is for you to take a drink of water when thirsty."

He warned white Americans that God's judgment was coming: "I tell you Americans! that unless you speedily alter your course, *you* and your *Country are gone*!!!!!! For God Almighty will tear up the very face of the earth!!!" The pamphlet's typography—its multiple exclamation points, capital letters, and italics—conveys the urgency and fury of a voice that would not be restrained.

Walker appealed also to the Declaration of Independence: "See your Declaration Americans!!! Do you understand your own language? Hear your language, proclaimed to the world, July 4th, 1776—'We hold these truths to be self evident—that ALL MEN ARE CREATED EQUAL!!'"

## Circulation and panic

Walker distributed the *Appeal* in the South by sewing copies into the linings of clothes he sold to sailors bound for Southern ports. Copies were found in **Savannah**, Georgia, in December 1829, and in Charleston, New Orleans, Richmond, and Wilmington. Southern legislatures responded with alarm. **Georgia** offered a reward of $1,000 for Walker dead, and $10,000 alive, according to reports; it passed laws quarantining Black sailors and making it a capital crime to circulate literature inciting slaves to rebellion. North Carolina and Louisiana passed similar laws, and strengthened laws against teaching slaves to read. The governor of Georgia wrote to the mayor of Boston demanding that Walker be stopped; the mayor replied that Walker had broken no law.

Walker published three editions of the *Appeal* in less than a year. In **August 1830**, he died suddenly in Boston. Rumors spread that he had been poisoned; the death record attributes it to tuberculosis. A year later, in **August 1831**, **Nat Turner** led his rebellion in Virginia, and Southerners blamed Walker's pamphlet, though there is no evidence that Turner read it.

## Maria Stewart

Walker's friend and admirer, **Maria W. Stewart** (1803–1879), carried on his work. A free Black woman born in Hartford, Connecticut, orphaned at five, and widowed in 1829, Stewart was inspired by Walker and by a religious conversion. In **1831**, she published a pamphlet, ***Religion and the Pure Principles of Morality***, and between **1832 and 1833** she delivered four public lectures in Boston—the first known public speeches by an American-born woman, Black or white, to a mixed audience of men and women on political subjects. She urged Black women to educate themselves and to demand their rights: "How long shall the fair daughters of Africa be compelled to bury their minds and talents beneath a load of iron pots and kettles?" She was attacked for speaking, and in her "**Farewell Address**" of **1833**, she defended women's right to speak publicly, citing the women of the Bible, before leaving Boston for New York. Her speeches were published by William Lloyd Garrison in *The Liberator*.

> **Pause and consider.** Walker addressed his *Appeal* to Black readers rather than to white ones. How does this choice of audience change what he can say?`,
